#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070028 :tutorial/content "What is the value of $x$ that satisfies the following equation?\n\n$$\n3^x = 3^4\n$$\n\nWe call this an **exponential equation** because the variable is contained within the exponent.\n\nNow, this equation is pretty easy to solve. Because the bases on both sides of the equation are the same (they are both equal to $3$), we can simply equate the powers.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3^{x} &= 3^{4} \\\\\nx &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to this equation is $x=4$." :tutorial/id #uuid "1fc010d0-e380-5035-bdb6-5d5d7cff060b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8837 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070029 :tutorial/content "Let's take a look at the following equation:\n\n$$\n5^x = 25\n$$\n\nWe can't solve this equation straight off the bat because the two sides of the equation don't share a common base.\n\nHowever, notice that $25$ can be written as a power of $5$:\n\n$$\n25=5^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite the equation as\n\n$$\n5^x = 5^2\n$$\n\nSince both sides of the equation are now written as an exponential expression with a common base of $5$, we can set the exponents equal to each other and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5^{x} &= 5^{2} \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo check that the solution is correct, we plug $x=2$ into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 5^{2} &= 25 \\\\ 25 &= 25 \\; {\\color{green}{\\checkmark}} \\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "43789602-55db-50d6-8517-6be1716d6133" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8839 :tutorial/title "Changing the Base of an Expression"}]}
